<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Mcs.Trace</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!--
      dotnet test walks the solution and skips anything that is not a test project, but the
      SDK prints an advisory line per project that has never said which it is. Saying so.
    -->
    <IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    No PackageReference, and that is a requirement rather than an accident. System.Reflection.
    Metadata, System.Xml.Linq and System.Text.RegularExpressions are all in Microsoft.NETCore.App
    on net10.0, so this tool adds nothing to the restore graph. Directory.Build.props audits every
    transitive package at severity low with warnings as errors: a dependency here would be a
    future build break in the one step whose job is to tell you the requirements table is stale,
    and a red gate nobody believes gets deleted.

    MetadataLoadContext would have been the comfortable way to read the attributes and is *not*
    in the shared framework: it ships as a package, and it also needs an assembly resolver over
    the runtime directory to load a test assembly's dependencies. MetadataReader needs neither,
    because nothing here is executed; the attribute is read as bytes.
  -->

</Project>
